ctor-agent".\n\nSo, the error logs mention connecting to 10.105.194.137:4317. Let me check if that IP corresponds to the Jaeger service. If the service name is "jaeger", then checking its endpoints might show the correct IP. Alternatively, maybe the OpenTelemetry Collector is deployed under a different name. \n\nSince the user can\'t find the otel-collector-agent service or deployment, perhaps the correct approach is to check all services in the namespace to see which one is handling OTLP. The product-catalog is trying to connect to OTLP, so the service should be named something like "otel-collector" or "jaeger". Let me use the get_services tool again to list all services and look for any related to OpenTelemetry or Jaeger. That way, the user can identify the correct service name and check its status.\n</think>\n\ntool name: get_services  \ntool description: Re-check the list of services in the \'astronomy-shop\' namespace to confirm the correct name of the OpenTelemetry Collector or Jaeger service, as previous attempts to access "otel-collector-agent" failed. This will ensure the service name is accurate before further investigation.  \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "get_services", "arguments": {}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 523, 'prompt_tokens': 4130, 'total_tokens': 4653, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd24-e348-70d0-9d05-7047b671dfdc-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4130, 'output_tokens': 523, 'total_tokens': 4653}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='4d77907a-109a-4635-acac-c64e57013fa9'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with the OpenTelemetry Collector in their Kubernetes cluster. The product-catalog service is having trouble connecting to the OTLP endpoint, which is supposed to be handled by the OpenTelemetry Collector. \n\nEarlier steps showed that the `otel-collector-agent` pods aren\'t running, and when they tried to check the deployment and service for that name, they got "not found" errors. So the service or deployment with that name doesn\'t exist. The user then tried to list all services in the namespace using `get_services`, which returned a list including \'jaeger\' and other services but not \'otel-collector-agent\'.\n\nWait, the initial `get_services` call in the first step gave a list of services, but maybe the correct service name is different. The user might have assumed the service was named \'otel-collector-agent\', but perhaps it\'s actually named something else, like \'jaeger\' or another name. The logs mentioned an IP address 10.105.194.137:4317, which might be the Jaeger service\'s endpoint. \n\nSo, the next step should be to check the services again to confirm the correct name. The user already called `get_services` again, but maybe they need to look for a service related to OpenTelemetry or Jaeger. If the service isn\'t found, perhaps the OpenTelemetry Collector is deployed under a different name, or maybe it\'s a different component altogether. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the service is misconfigured, pointing to a non-existent pod. The user should verify the service\'s YAML to see the target pods or deployments. But since the service name isn\'t found, the first priority is to confirm the correct service name. If the service isn\'t listed, then the deployment might be missing or incorrectly named. \n\nThe user might need to check the deployments as well. If the service isn\'t found, perhaps the deployment for the OpenTelemetry Collector isn\'t properly set up. But since the user can\'t find the service, they need to first ensure they\'re using the correct name. \n\nIn the previous steps, the user tried `otel-collector-agent` and it wasn\'t found. The `get_services` call returned a list that included \'jaeger\', which is a different service. Maybe the OTLP endpoint is actually the Jaeger service, and the product-catalog is misconfigured to point to the wrong service. \n\nSo, the next step is to check the Jaeger service\'s configuration to see if it\'s set up to receive OTLP traces. If Jaeger is the correct service, then the product-catalog\'s configuration might be incorrect, pointing to the wrong endpoint. Alternatively, the OpenTelemetry Collector might be deployed under a different name, and the user needs to find that. \n\nBut since the user can\'t find the service, they might need to check all services again or look for any service related to OpenTelemetry. If the service isn\'t present, then the deployment might be missing, leading to the connection refusal error. \n\nIn summary, the user needs to confirm the correct service name for the OpenTelemetry Collector or Jaeger, check its configuration, and ensure it\'s running. If the service isn\'t found, the deployment might be misconfigured or missing, leading to the connection issue.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{}', 'name': 'get_services'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9be07a94bc6973aa', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 719, 'prompt_tokens': 5115, 'total_tokens': 5834, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd24-fbc4-7ea1-8b76-bc3aac1f7dcb-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_services', 'args': {}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9be07a94bc6973aa', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5115, 'output_tokens': 719, 'total_tokens': 5834}), ToolMessage(content='meta=None content=[TextContent(type=\'text\', text="[\'ad\', \'frontend-proxy\', \'image-provider\', \'product-catalog\', \'jaeger-all-in-one\', \'load-generator\', \'flagd\', \'frontend\', \'cart\']", annotations=None)] isError=False', id='3d522946-7838-4a2d-abdf-6b784f907b46', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-9be07a94bc6973aa'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='6cc5cc82-a526-49da-b9c3-bebf90e00b81'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application. The problem seems to be related to the product-catalog service not being able to connect to an OTLP endpoint. Earlier attempts to check the otel-collector-agent service and deployment didn\'t find anything, and the service name might be incorrect.\n\nIn the latest step, the user called get_services again and got a list of services, which includes \'jaeger-all-in-one\'. Wait, earlier logs mentioned an IP address 10.105.194.137:4317, which is the OTLP endpoint. Maybe the correct service name is \'jaeger-all-in-one\' instead of \'otel-collector-agent\'. Jaeger is a distributed tracing system, and it\'s possible that the OpenTelemetry Collector is configured to send data to Jaeger. \n\nSo, the next step should be to check the Jaeger service\'s configuration. The user should verify if the Jaeger service is running and if it\'s properly set up to receive OTLP data. They can use kubectl to get the pods and services related to Jaeger. Also, checking the logs of the Jaeger pod might show if there are any issues with the service listening on port 4317. \n\nAlternatively, the user could check the service\'s YAML to see the target port and endpoints. If the Jaeger service is not correctly pointing to the pod, that could cause the connection refusal. Also, ensuring that the OpenTelemetry Collector is configured to send data to the correct Jaeger service address. \n\nSo, the next tool call should be to get the Jaeger service\'s details using kubectl, maybe get service jaeger-all-in-one to check its spec. Also, checking the pods related to Jaeger to see if they are running. If the service is misconfigured, that would explain why the product-catalog can\'t connect.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \ntool description: The `get_services` output shows the Jaeger service is named `jaeger-all-in-one`. The earlier error logs reference an OTLP endpoint at IP `10.105.194.137:4317`, which likely corresponds to Jaeger\'s OTLP receiver. I will now check the Jaeger pod logs to confirm if it is running and listening on port 4317, as this would explain the connection refusal.  \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl logs jaeger-all-in-one-7d6f586f64-2xgkl -n astronomy-shop"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 564, 'prompt_tokens': 4340, 'total_tokens': 4904, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd25-1e9e-7712-8219-9f7448529814-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4340, 'output_tokens': 564, 'total_tokens': 4904}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='65578772-cae1-41b3-9c89-1fe8ae0aa42f'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what's going on here. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application. The main problem seems to be that the product-catalog service is having trouble connecting to an OTLP endpoint, which is causing it to restart. \n\nFirst, they checked the pods in the namespace and found that product-catalog had two restarts. Then they looked at the logs and saw errors about connection refusal to port 4317, which is the OTLP port. That points to the OpenTelemetry Collector or Jaeger not being available.\n\nThey tried checking the otel-collector-agent pods and services but couldn't find them. Then they checked the services list again and found that the correct Jaeger service is named jaeger-all-in-one. So the previous attempts to access otel-collector-agent were incorrect.\n\nNow, they're looking at the logs of the jaeger-all-in-one pod. The logs might show if Jaeger is running properly and if it's listening on the correct port. If Jaeger isn't running or the port isn't open, that would explain why product-catalog can't connect. \n\nThe next step is to check the Jaeger pod's status and logs. If Jaeger is down or not configured correctly, that's the root cause. They might also need to check the service configuration for jaeger-all-in-one to ensure it's exposing the correct port and that the service is properly linked to the pod. \n\nIf the logs show that Jaeger is running but not accepting connections, there could be a network policy issue or a misconfiguration in the service. Alternatively, if the pod is crashing, looking into its logs would reveal why it's not starting up correctly. \n\nSo, the user should check the Jaeger pod logs to confirm its status and ensure that the OTLP endpoint is correctly set up. If Jaeger is the issue, they might need to restart the pod or check its configuration. If the service is misconfigured, adjusting the service YAML to correctly expose the port and target the right pod would resolve the connection problem.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl describe pod jaeger-all-in-one-7d6f586f64-2xgkl -n astronomy-shop"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-90b541f970af9973', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 487, 'prompt_tokens': 5404, 'total_tokens': 5891, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd25-3914-78e0-bbc9-1420ff7654b5-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl describe pod jaeger-all-in-one-7d6f586f64-2xgkl -n astronomy-shop'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-90b541f970af9973', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5404, 'output_tokens': 487, 'total_tokens': 5891}), ToolMessage(content='Command Rejected: Error executing kubectl command:\nError from server (NotFound): pods "jaeger-all-in-one-7d6f586f64-2xgkl" not found\n', id='de5322a1-aeb4-4b49-a468-db9c8a0efb10', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-90b541f970af9973'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='c54bf7af-866c-4453-83d3-c6c8280d1097'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application. The main problem seems to be that the product-catalog service is failing to connect to an OTLP endpoint, leading to connection refused errors. \n\nEarlier steps showed that the otel-collector-agent pods weren\'t found, and the jaeger-all-in-one service exists but the specific pod wasn\'t found. The user tried to get the pod logs but got a Not Found error. \n\nNow, the next step should be to check if the Jaeger pod is actually running. Since the previous attempt to describe the pod failed, maybe the pod name is incorrect or the service isn\'t properly configured. The user should first verify the list of pods in the namespace to find the correct Jaeger pod name. \n\nUsing the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool with "kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop" would list all pods, including Jaeger. Then, the correct pod name can be identified. Once the correct name is known, checking its status and logs would help determine if Jaeger is running and listening on the required port. \n\nAlternatively, checking the service configuration for jaeger-all-in-one to see if it\'s correctly pointing to the pod\'s IP and port. If the service is misconfigured, that could cause the connection issues. Also, ensuring that the OTLP endpoint is properly set up in the product-catalog service\'s configuration to point to the correct Jaeger service.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \ntool description: The `jaeger-all-in-one` service exists, but the specific pod name used earlier was invalid. To resolve this, I will first list all pods in the namespace to identify the correct Jaeger pod name. This ensures accurate logging and inspection of the Jaeger service\'s status.  \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 406, 'prompt_tokens': 4652, 'total_tokens': 5058, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd25-537a-7e40-82aa-5120f96ce598-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4652, 'output_tokens': 406, 'total_tokens': 5058}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='27eb1d8f-8c98-4b17-9608-02a03c6df540'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application. The main problem seems to be that the product-catalog service is failing to connect to an OTLP endpoint, leading to connection refused errors. \n\nFirst, they checked the pods and found that product-catalog had some restarts. Then they looked at the logs and saw errors related to the OTLP exporter. The IP address mentioned in the logs, 10.105.194.137:4317, is likely the Jaeger service's OTLP endpoint. \n\nThey tried checking the otel-collector-agent pods and service, but those weren't found. Then they used get_services and found that the correct Jaeger service name is jaeger-all-in-one. However, when they tried to get the pod logs for jaeger-all-in-one, the pod name was invalid. \n\nSo now, the user needs to find the correct pod name for jaeger-all-in-one. The previous kubectl get pods command listed all pods, but the specific pod name used was incorrect. The next step is to check the list of pods again to find the correct name of the Jaeger pod. Once they have the correct pod name, they can check its logs to see if Jaeger is running and listening on port 4317. If Jaeger isn't running or the port isn't open, that would explain why the product-catalog service can't connect, leading to the connection refused error. \n\nTherefore, the next tool call should be to get the list of pods again to identify the correct Jaeger pod name. This will allow them to check the Jaeger service's status and logs, which should help determine the root cause of the connection issue.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b909a84082d18884', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 401, 'prompt_tokens': 5657, 'total_t