2026-03-17 18:55:33 - DEBUG - all.stratus.diagnosis - [Loop 16] Tool call - base_agent.py:llm_tool_call_step:102 [Full Prompt = [SystemMessage(content="Monitor and diagnose an application consisting of **MANY** microservices. Some or none of the microservices have faults. Get all the pods and deployments to figure out what kind of services are running in the cluster.  Carefully identify the whether the faults are present and if they are, and identify what is the root cause of the fault.\nStop diagnosis once you've found the root cause of the faults.\nGo as deep as you can into what is causing the issue.\nYour instructions to the tools must be clear and concise. Your queries to tools need to be single turn.\nRemember to check these, and remember this information: ## Workloads (Applications) - **Pod**: The smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes, representing a single instance of a running application. Can contain one or more tightly coupled containers. - **ReplicaSet**: Ensures that a specified number of pod replicas are running at all times. Often managed indirectly through Deployments. - **Deployment**: Manages the deployment and lifecycle of applications. Provides declarative updates for Pods and ReplicaSets. - **StatefulSet**: Manages stateful applications with unique pod identities and stable storage. Used for workloads like databases. - **DaemonSet**: Ensures that a copy of a specific pod runs on every node in the cluster. Useful for node monitoring agents, log collectors, etc. - **Job**: Manages batch processing tasks that are expected to complete successfully. Ensures pods run to completion. - **CronJob**: Schedules jobs to run at specified times or intervals (similar to cron in Linux).\n## Networking - **Service**: Provides a stable network endpoint for accessing a group of pods. Types: ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer, and ExternalName. - **Ingress**: Manages external HTTP(S) access to services in the cluster. Supports routing and load balancing for HTTP(S) traffic. - **NetworkPolicy**: Defines rules for network communication between pods and other entities. Used for security and traffic control.\n## Storage - **PersistentVolume (PV)**: Represents a piece of storage in the cluster, provisioned by an administrator or dynamically. - **PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)**: Represents a request for storage by a user. Binds to a PersistentVolume. - **StorageClass**: Defines different storage tiers or backends for dynamic provisioning of PersistentVolumes. - **ConfigMap**: Stores configuration data as key-value pairs for applications. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive data like passwords, tokens, or keys in an encrypted format.\n## Configuration and Metadata - **Namespace**: Logical partitioning of resources within the cluster for isolation and organization. - **ConfigMap**: Provides non-sensitive configuration data in key-value format. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive configuration data securely. - **ResourceQuota**: Restricts resource usage (e.g., CPU, memory) within a namespace. - **LimitRange**: Enforces minimum and maximum resource limits for containers in a namespace.\n## Cluster Management - **Node**: Represents a worker machine in the cluster (virtual or physical). Runs pods and is managed by the control plane. - **ClusterRole and Role**: Define permissions for resources at the cluster or namespace level. - **ClusterRoleBinding and RoleBinding**: Bind roles to users or groups for authorization. - **ServiceAccount**: Associates processes in pods with permissions for accessing the Kubernetes API.\nAfter you finished, submit the detected faulty components to the orchestrator for evaluation. The submission should be a natural language description of the root cause of the failure.\n", additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='93be1426-495e-402e-86ab-99941d200c88'), HumanMessage(content="You will be working this application:\nOpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop\nHere are some descriptions about the application:\nAn online shopping platform built with a microservices architecture, showcasing OpenTelemetry instrumentation for distributed tracing across services.\nIt belongs to this namespace:\nastronomy-shop\nIn each round, there is a thinking stage. In the thinking stage, you are given a list of tools. Think about what you want to call. Return your tool choice and the reasoning behind When choosing the tool, refer to the tool by its name. Then, there is a tool-call stage, where you make a tool_call consistent with your explanation. You can run up to 20 rounds to finish the tasks. If you call submit_tool in tool-call stage, the process will end immediately. If you exceed this limitation, the system will force you to make a submission. You will begin by analyzing the service's state and telemetry with the tools.\n", additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='fa3895cd-dcd6-4fc7-acb8-e2505a419662'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Here are all the tools you can use:\ntool name: get_traces\n\ntool description: Get Jaeger traces for a given service in the last n minutes.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service for which to retrieve trace data.\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n\n\ntool name: get_services\n\ntool description: Retrieve the list of service names from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of service names available in Grafana.\n\n\ntool name: get_operations\n\ntool description: Query available operations for a specific service from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service whose operations should be retrieved.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of operation names associated with the specified service.\n\n\ntool name: get_dependency_graph\n\ntool description: Get service dependency graph from Jaeger\'s native dependencies API.\n    Args:\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n    Returns:\n        str: JSON object representing the dependency graph.\n\n\ntool name: get_metrics\n\ntool description: Query real-time metrics data from the Prometheus instance.\n\n    Args:\n        query (str): A Prometheus Query Language (PromQL) expression used to fetch metric values.\n\n    Returns:\n        dict: The raw Prometheus response containing metric results, including timestamps, values, and labels.\n\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\n\ntool description: This is a tool used to execute read-only kubectl commands.\n\n    Args:\n        command (str): The read-only kubectl command you want to execute in a CLI to manage a k8s cluster. It should start with "kubectl".\n        Available Read-only Commands:\n        "kubectl api-resources",\n        "kubectl api-version",\n        "kubectl auth can-i",\n        "kubectl cluster-info",\n        "kubectl describe",\n        "kubectl diff",\n        "kubectl events",\n        "kubectl explain",\n        "kubectl get",\n        "kubectl logs",\n        "kubectl options",\n        "kubectl top",\n        "kubectl version",\n        "kubectl config view",\n        "kubectl config current-context",\n        "kubectl config get",\n        INTERACTIVE COMMANDS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g. "kubectl edit"\n        INTERACTIVE FLAGS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g., "kubectl logs -f"\n\n\ntool name: submit_tool\n\ntool description: The tool to submit benchmark results\n\n    Args:\n        ans (str): the answer you would like to submit to the benchmark. Use natural language to describe the root cause of the failure.\n\n\nChoose a tool from the list and output the tool name. Justify your tool choice. In the next step, you will generate a tool call for this tool', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='3bf168bf-63e7-4425-91f6-bc445ccf9f17'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's start by understanding the problem. The user wants me to monitor and diagnose an application called OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop, which is a microservices-based platform. The goal is to identify any faults and determine their root causes. \n\nFirst, I need to figure out what services are running in the cluster. The application is in the namespace 'astronomy-shop'. The tools available include 'get_services' which retrieves service names from Grafana. That seems like a good starting point because knowing the services will help me check each one for issues.\n\nSo, I'll use 'get_services' to list all the services in the Grafana instance. Once I have the list, I can then check each service's metrics, traces, and dependencies to see if there are any anomalies. For example, if a service is down or has high error rates, that could indicate a problem. \n\nAfter getting the services, maybe I'll use 'get_metrics' to look at real-time metrics like error rates or latency. If there are spikes in errors or high latency, that could point to faulty services. Then, using 'get_traces' for specific services to see if there are any trace data indicating issues like failed requests or long durations. \n\nAlso, the 'get_dependency_graph' could show dependencies between services, helping to identify if a problem in one service is affecting others. If a service isn't showing up in the dependency graph, that might mean it's not running or has issues. \n\nBut first, I need the list of services. So the first step is to call 'get_services' to get all the service names in the 'astronomy-shop' namespace. That way, I can systematically check each service for potential faults.\n</think>\n\nget_services  \nTo begin diagnosing the application, I need to identify all the services running in the 'astronomy-shop' namespace. The `get_services` tool will retrieve the list of service names from Grafana, providing a foundation for further analysis of their health, metrics, and dependencies. This is the first step in understanding the application's architecture and identifying potential fault points.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 432, 'prompt_tokens': 1492, 'total_tokens': 1924, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd23-a7f2-7bc3-94ea-56bf92e14798-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1492, 'output_tokens': 432, 'total_tokens': 1924}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='2e3f5b0d-3cba-47d0-b38b-9b215d716d19'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user wants me to monitor and diagnose an application with many microservices. The application is called OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop, and it\'s in the namespace astronomy-shop. The goal is to figure out if there are any faults and identify the root cause.\n\nIn the previous step, I chose the get_services tool to retrieve the list of service names from Grafana. Now, I need to generate a tool call for that. The get_services tool doesn\'t require any arguments, so the command should be straightforward. The response will give me the list of services, which is essential to know which services are part of the application. Once I have the service names, I can proceed to check each one\'s health, metrics, and dependencies. \n\nWait, but the user mentioned that some or none of the microservices might have faults. So, after getting the services, the next step would be to check their metrics, maybe look into logs, or check their dependencies. But first, I need the list of services. The get_services tool is the right choice here. Let me make sure I\'m using the correct tool and parameters. The tool doesn\'t need any parameters, so the call should be just invoking get_services. \n\nI should also remember that the user is in the thinking stage, and the next step is to generate the tool call. So the correct JSON object would be {"name": "get_services", "arguments": {}}. That\'s all. No arguments needed. Once I get the services, I can move on to other tools like get_metrics or get_dependency_graph. But for now, the first step is to list all services in the namespace. \n\nWait, but the get_services tool\'s description says it retrieves service names from Grafana. Does that mean it\'s specific to the astronomy-shop namespace? The user mentioned the namespace, but the tool\'s description doesn\'t specify. Maybe I should check if there\'s a way to filter by namespace, but according to the tool\'s parameters, there\'s no namespace argument. Hmm. Maybe the Grafana instance is already scoped to the namespace, or perhaps the tool returns all services in the cluster. But the user specified the namespace, so maybe I need to use kubectl to get services in that namespace. Wait, but the available tools include exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd. \n\nWait, the user\'s initial instructions say to use the provided tools. The get_services tool is part of the available tools. But if the get_services tool doesn\'t take a namespace parameter, maybe it\'s not filtering correctly. However, the user mentioned the namespace is astronomy-shop, so perhaps the Grafana instance is already set to that namespace, or the tool\'s response includes services from that namespace. \n\nBut since the tool\'s parameters don\'t include a namespace, I have to assume that the get_services tool returns all services in the cluster. However, the user is focusing on the astronomy-shop namespace. Maybe I should first use kubectl to get the services in that namespace. But the available tools include exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd. So perhaps I should use that to run \'kubectl get services -n astronomy-shop\' to get the list. \n\nWait, but the user\'s initial step was to use get_services. Maybe the get_services tool is designed to get services from Grafana, which might be a different source. But the user\'s application is in a specific namespace. This is a bit confusing. However, the user\'s first step was to use get_services, so maybe I should proceed with that. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the get_services tool is part of the monitoring stack and returns services that are being monitored. But without more info, I have to follow the user\'s initial choice. So the correct tool call is get_services with no arguments. \n\nSo the tool call would be:\n\n{"name": "get_services", "arguments": {}}\n\nThat\'s the correct step here. Once I have the list of services, I can then check each one\'s metrics, dependencies, and logs. But for now, the first step is to get the services.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{}', 'name': 'get_services'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8e8dac4729926fbc', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 852, 'prompt_tokens': 2473, 'total_tokens': 3325, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd23-bcca-7570-a902-f320a5c31d92-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_services', 'args': {}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8e8dac4729926fbc', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2473, 'output_tokens': 852, 'total_tokens': 3325}), ToolMessage(content='meta=None content=[TextContent(type=\'text\', text="[\'image-provider\', \'cart\', \'product-catalog\', \'ad\', \'frontend-proxy\', \'load-generator\', \'flagd\', \'frontend\']", annotations=None)] isError=False', id='aa9e9f84-91b9-4394-af9e-cace1221fa9e', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-8e8dac4729926fbc'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='f1ba75aa-2591-4fec-9c88-2f8cebac65fc'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, so the user is trying to diagnose an issue in the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application. They\'ve already used the get_services tool to list all the services in the namespace. The next step is to figure out which of these services might be having problems.\n\nFirst, I need to recall the tools available. There\'s get_traces, get_dependency_graph, get_metrics, and others. Since the user mentioned that some or none of the microservices have faults, I should probably check for any anomalies in their metrics or traces. \n\nThe get_dependency_graph tool could show how services are interconnected, which might reveal if there\'s a service causing a cascade of failures. Alternatively, using get_traces for each service might help identify specific issues like slow requests or errors. But since the user wants to go deep, maybe starting with the dependency graph would give an overview of the system\'s health. \n\nWait, the get_dependency_graph tool uses Jaeger\'s native dependencies API. If there are services that are not communicating properly, the graph might show missing links or errors. But I\'m not sure if that\'s the best first step. Alternatively, checking metrics with get_metrics could show if any services are underperforming. For example, looking at error rates or latency metrics. \n\nBut the user hasn\'t specified any particular symptoms, like errors or slow performance. So maybe the first step is to check the dependency graph to see the overall structure and see if any services are isolated or not functioning as expected. Alternatively, using get_traces for each service with a recent time frame might uncover issues. \n\nWait, the user wants to find the root cause. If I start with the dependency graph, it might show which services are critical and if there are any bottlenecks. But without knowing which service is faulty, it\'s a bit vague. Maybe a better approach is to check the metrics for each service. For example, using get_metrics with PromQL queries to check for errors or high latency. \n\nBut the user hasn\'t provided specific metrics. So perhaps the next step is to use get_traces on each service to look for any errors or anomalies in their traces. However, that might be time-consuming. Alternatively, using get_dependency_graph could help identify which services are dependent on others, so if one service is down, it affects others. \n\nHmm. The user might need to start by checking the health of each service. Since the get_services tool gave a list, maybe the next step is to check the status of each service\'s pods. Using the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with "kubectl get pods" in the astronomy-shop namespace would show if any pods are in a failed state. That\'s a more direct way to check for immediate issues. \n\nWait, the user hasn\'t mentioned any specific symptoms, so maybe the first step is to check the pod statuses. If any pods are crashing or not running, that\'s a clear sign of a problem. So using the kubectl get pods command in the specified namespace would be a good next step. That would help identify if there are any immediately visible issues with the pods. \n\nBut the user\'s tools include exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd. So the correct command would be to run "kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop" to list all pods in that namespace. That would show if any pods are in CrashLoopBackOff, Error, or other problematic states. \n\nYes, that makes sense. So the next tool to use is exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command "kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop". This will provide the current state of all pods, which is crucial for identifying any immediate issues. Once that\'s done, if any pods are problematic, further steps can be taken to investigate those specific pods.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nTo identify potential faults, I need to check the current state of all pods in the \'astronomy-shop\' namespace. The `exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd` tool will execute `kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop`, revealing if any pods are in a non-Running state (e.g., CrashLoopBackOff, Error). This provides immediate visibility into pod-level failures, which is critical for diagnosing microservice issues.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 860, 'prompt_tokens': 1691, 'total_tokens': 2551, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd23-e528-71f2-9dcb-4824598d7720-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1691, 'output_tokens': 860, 'total_tokens': 2551}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='c3d16163-47ca-4d3f-8bb8-f745aa379b7b'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose an application with many microservices. They mentioned the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop in the namespace \'astronomy-shop\'. So far, they\'ve used get_services to list the services and now they want to check the pod status.\n\nThe next step is to use exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command \'kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop\'. That makes sense because checking pod statuses is crucial. If any pods are not in the Running state, that could indicate the root cause. The user needs to see if there are any crashed or erroring pods. \n\nWait, the available commands include \'kubectl get\', so the command should be "kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop". The tool requires the command to start with \'kubectl\', which it does. The namespace is specified correctly. This command will list all pods in the namespace, showing their status. \n\nBy running this, they can quickly spot any pods that are not running, which would be a sign of faults. For example, if a pod is in CrashLoopBackOff, that means it\'s failing to start. Or if it\'s in Error, there\'s an issue with the container. This is a fundamental check before moving on to metrics or traces. \n\nSo the tool call should be exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command "kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop". That\'s the right approach to start investigating potential pod-level issues.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-88f436e450dbcf73', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 329, 'prompt_tokens': 2689, 'total_tokens': 3018, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd24-0c34-7012-8c6b-e34d8a6f5446-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-88f436e450dbcf73', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2689, 'output_tokens': 329, 'total_tokens': 3018}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                               READY   STATUS    RESTARTS        AGE\naccounting-844d99ddff-xztgp        1/1     Running   0               2m53s\nad-6f9964ffd8-qcddv                1/1     Running   0               2m53s\ncart-65474cd577-ds4dp              1/1     Running   0               2m53s\ncheckout-648b497649-tgg82          1/1     Running   0               2m52s\ncurrency-69f5b55bbc-sfl2k          1/1     Running   0               2m53s\nemail-56985bb4f8-kpnwc             1/1     Running   0               2m53s\nflagd-79c4c6cb44-m7stj             2/2     Running   0  